      <p class="content-hero-updated">Updated &middot; January 2025</p>
      <h1 class="content-hero-title">Mescaline</h1>
      <p class="content-hero-subtitle">Mescaline is a naturally occurring phenethylamine found primarily in peyote (<em>Lophophora williamsii</em>) and San Pedro cactus (<em>Echinopsis pachanoi</em>). It is one of the oldest known psychedelic substances &mdash; used ceremonially by Indigenous peoples of the Americas for thousands of years. This guide covers mescaline&rsquo;s pharmacology, effects, the critical ethical dimensions of non-Indigenous engagement, and harm reduction.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- At a Glance HUD -->
    <SubstanceHud id="mescaline" />

    <!-- Table of Contents (sidebar on desktop) -->
    <div class="article-layout">
      <SubstanceNav id="mescaline" />

      <div class="article-body">

        <!-- What Mescaline Is -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-what">What Mescaline Is</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline (3,4,5-trimethoxyphenethylamine) is a naturally occurring phenethylamine alkaloid that acts as a 5-HT2A receptor agonist &mdash; the same primary target as psilocybin and LSD, though mescaline&rsquo;s broader receptor binding profile and phenethylamine structure give it a distinct experiential character. It was first isolated and identified by German pharmacologist Arthur Heffter in 1897 and was the first psychedelic to be chemically identified in the Western scientific tradition.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline is the primary psychoactive alkaloid in peyote, where it occurs alongside dozens of other alkaloids whose contributions to the overall experience are not fully understood. San Pedro cactus contains mescaline in lower concentrations but is more widely available and less ecologically precarious. Synthetic mescaline exists but is uncommon.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Peyote vs. San Pedro -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-sources">Peyote vs. San Pedro: An Important Distinction</h2>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Peyote (<em>Lophophora williamsii</em>)</strong> grows slowly &mdash; a single button can take 10&ndash;30 years to mature &mdash; and is found primarily in the Chihuahuan Desert of Mexico and the Rio Grande region of Texas. It is under serious ecological pressure from overharvesting, driven in part by the global expansion of interest in mescaline among non-Indigenous users. Peyote is deeply sacred to multiple Indigenous peoples, most notably within the ceremonies of the Native American Church and traditional Huichol (Wix&aacute;ritari) practice in Mexico.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>San Pedro (<em>Echinopsis pachanoi</em>)</strong> grows much more quickly, is widely cultivated as an ornamental plant, and does not carry the same ecological or cultural weight as peyote. For non-Indigenous people interested in mescaline, San Pedro is the far more ethically appropriate choice &mdash; it does not deplete protected sacred plant populations or trespass on ceremonial traditions that belong to specific Indigenous communities.</p>
          <div class="content-callout">
            <div class="callout-label">Peyote conservation</div>
            <p>Peyote populations in their native range are in serious decline. The combination of slow growth, habitat loss, and harvesting pressure means that wild peyote is genuinely threatened. Non-Indigenous use of peyote &mdash; beyond its established ceremonial contexts &mdash; contributes to this pressure. Many Indigenous voices have asked non-Indigenous people explicitly to refrain from seeking peyote.</p>
          </div>
          <NatureCard id="mescaline" />
        </div>

        <!-- History & Cultural Roots -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-history">History &amp; Cultural Roots</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline has the longest documented human relationship of any substance covered on this site. Specimens recovered from a rock shelter on the Rio Grande in Texas, made from ground peyote and preserved in a museum collection for decades before anyone analysed them, were radiocarbon dated to roughly 5,700 years ago and still contained mescaline when tested in 2005. People in that region were preparing peyote before writing existed anywhere.</p>
          <p class="content-p">In the Andes the record is separately ancient. San Pedro appears in the stone carvings at Chav&iacute;n de Hu&aacute;ntar in Peru from around three thousand years ago, held in the hand of a carved figure, and the plant has remained in continuous use in northern Peruvian healing practice ever since.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Spanish colonial authorities understood exactly what they were looking at and moved against it. The Inquisition prohibited peyote in 1620, classifying its use as superstition and an act against the faith. Suppression continued in various forms for three centuries and did not work. The Wix&aacute;ritari of the Sierra Madre have maintained their annual pilgrimage to Wirikuta in San Luis Potos&iacute; throughout, walking hundreds of kilometres to gather hikuri in the place their cosmology holds the world began.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Peyotism and the Native American Church</h3>
          <p class="content-p">During the late nineteenth century, as Plains nations were confined to reservations and their own ceremonies were banned, peyote practice spread north from Mexico and the Texas borderlands and took a new form. It combined older ceremonial structure with Christian elements, and it spread quickly because it worked as a means of holding communities together under conditions designed to break them. Quanah Parker, the Comanche leader, was among its most influential advocates. In 1918 practitioners incorporated formally as the Native American Church, partly as legal protection against the campaigns to suppress it.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Legal protection stayed contested for most of the twentieth century. In 1990 the United States Supreme Court held in <em>Employment Division v. Smith</em> that a state could deny unemployment benefits to men dismissed for ceremonial peyote use, a decision that stripped away much of the protection practitioners had relied on. Congress responded in 1994 by amending the American Indian Religious Freedom Act to protect the use, possession, and transport of peyote by Native American practitioners for bona fide ceremonial purposes. That amendment is the basis of the legal position described under <a href="#mes-legal" class="inline-link">legal status</a>.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The Western encounter</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline was the first psychedelic the West isolated and named. The German pharmacologist Arthur Heffter identified it in 1897 by the direct method of taking the fractions himself until one of them worked, and Ernst Sp&auml;th synthesised it in 1919, making it the first psychedelic ever produced in a laboratory. Havelock Ellis wrote a widely read account of his own experience in 1898.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The book most people have heard of came later. Aldous Huxley took mescaline in 1953 under the supervision of the psychiatrist Humphry Osmond and published <em>The Doors of Perception</em> the following year. It shaped how a generation of English-speaking readers thought about these substances, and it is worth noticing that it did so on the basis of a single afternoon with a compound whose Indigenous context Huxley barely addressed.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Ceremony & Container -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-ceremony">Ceremony and Container</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline has living ceremonial containers, and a reader should understand two things about them: what they are, and that most of them are not open to you.</p>
          <p class="content-p">A Native American Church meeting typically runs from sundown to sunrise inside a tipi, around a fire, with defined roles. A roadman leads, a fireman tends the fire through the night, and there are songs accompanied by water drum and gourd rattle passing around the circle. Peyote is taken and passed. There is a water ceremony at midnight and again at dawn, and the meeting ends with a morning meal. The structure is prayer rather than exploration, and it is usually held with a specific purpose, often for a person who is ill or in difficulty.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Wix&aacute;rika practice centres on the pilgrimage rather than a single night, and it is bound to a specific landscape and a set of relationships between deer, maize, and peyote that does not translate out of that setting.</p>
          <p class="content-p">In northern Peru, huachuma or San Pedro ceremonies are led by curanderos working with a mesa, an arranged altar of objects, and often run through the night. This tradition has absorbed centuries of Catholic and colonial influence while remaining continuous, and it is the mescaline container non-Indigenous people are most likely to be able to enter honestly.</p>
          <div class="content-callout">
            <div class="callout-label">Most of these are not open containers</div>
            <p>The United States peyote exemption applies to Native American practitioners, and Native American Church membership is generally not open to non-Native people. Ceremonies advertised to outsiders as peyote meetings are usually operating outside both the law and the tradition they invoke. This is a real difference from ayahuasca, where a retreat sector exists whatever its problems. For mescaline, there is often no equivalent legitimate door.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Ethics, Indigenous Sovereignty -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-ethics">Ethics, Indigenous Sovereignty, and Non-Indigenous Engagement</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Peyote is a sacrament &mdash; not a recreational drug or a clinical tool in the Western sense &mdash; for the Native American Church and for Wix&aacute;ritari and other Indigenous peoples of Mexico and the American Southwest. The relationship between these peoples and peyote is covenantal: it belongs to a living cosmology, a set of obligations and relationships that cannot be extracted from the plant and transplanted into other contexts without profound loss of meaning.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Non-Indigenous interest in peyote has grown dramatically alongside the broader psychedelic renaissance. The consequences include: accelerating depletion of wild peyote populations; exploitation of ceremonial contexts by operators with no genuine relationship to the traditions; and continued legal criminalisation of Indigenous ceremonial use in some jurisdictions while non-Indigenous retreat tourism proceeds with relative impunity.</p>
          <p class="content-p">San Pedro carries a different &mdash; though not absent &mdash; ethical weight. Its Andean ceremonial uses (huachuma tradition) are living practices, and the same principles of respectful, reciprocal, non-extractive engagement apply. The key question is always: am I engaging with this tradition as a guest who understands their position, or as a consumer extracting an experience from a cultural product?</p>
          <p class="content-p">For most readers this resolves into one practical choice, and it is covered in <a href="#mes-considering" class="inline-link">if you are considering this</a>: San Pedro rather than peyote, for reasons that are ecological and ethical at once.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- How Mescaline Works -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-how">How Mescaline Works</h2>

          <h3 class="content-h3">A phenethylamine, not a tryptamine</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Psilocybin, LSD, and DMT are all tryptamines, sharing an indole ring inherited from the same structural family as serotonin. Mescaline is not. It is a phenethylamine, built on the same simple backbone as dopamine and norepinephrine, with three methoxy groups attached to the ring (hence 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenethylamine). This matters practically as well as chemically: the entire 2C family, including <a href="/twocb/" class="inline-link">2C-B</a>, consists of ring-substituted phenethylamines that Alexander Shulgin developed by working outward from the mescaline scaffold. Mescaline is the parent compound of a whole branch of psychedelic chemistry.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Despite the different structure, the primary target is the same one every classical psychedelic converges on: mescaline is a partial agonist at the serotonin 5-HT2A receptor, with additional activity at 5-HT2C and 5-HT1A. This shared mechanism is why mescaline shows cross-tolerance with psilocybin and LSD, so taking one shortly after another produces markedly blunted effects, even though the molecules look nothing alike.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Why the doses are so large</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline is by a wide margin the least potent of the classical psychedelics by weight. An active dose is measured in hundreds of milligrams, against roughly 20&ndash;25 mg for psilocybin and around 100 micrograms for LSD, a difference of several thousandfold between mescaline and LSD. This says nothing about intensity. A full mescaline dose is a full psychedelic experience. It reflects binding affinity: mescaline holds relatively weakly at 5-HT2A, so far more molecules are required to produce comparable receptor occupancy.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The practical consequence is bulk. Because the compound is weak by weight and the cacti that contain it are mostly water and fibre, a ceremonial dose means consuming a substantial quantity of plant material, which is a large part of why nausea is such a consistent feature of the onset, and why preparations are traditionally reduced by long boiling.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Metabolism, duration, and the MAOI interaction</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline's 10&ndash;12 hour duration comes from slow clearance. A substantial fraction is excreted from the body unchanged in urine, and most of the remainder is broken down by monoamine oxidase into 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenylacetic acid. That second route is the reason MAOI antidepressants are flagged as a serious interaction on this page and in the panel above: inhibiting monoamine oxidase removes a primary means of clearing the drug, which can intensify and considerably prolong an experience that is already long.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The other alkaloids, and the limits of what is known</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline is the principal psychoactive compound in peyote but not the only one. The cactus contains dozens of additional alkaloids, among them hordenine, pellotine, anhalonine and lophophorine. Some, such as pellotine, have sedative properties of their own. Whether and how this mixture shapes the character of a peyote experience relative to purified mescaline has not been properly characterised. Practitioners consistently report a difference. The pharmacology to explain it does not yet exist.</p>
          <p class="content-p">A broader caveat applies to this whole section. The network-level accounts of psychedelic action described on our <a href="/science/" class="inline-link">Science</a> page, such as default mode network suppression, the REBUS model and changes in cortical connectivity, were built almost entirely from neuroimaging studies of psilocybin and LSD. Mescaline has attracted very little modern imaging research, largely because a 12-hour session is expensive and demanding to run. It is reasonable to assume broadly similar network effects given the shared 5-HT2A mechanism, but that assumption has not been tested directly, and it should be held as an inference rather than a finding.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Effects -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-effects">Effects</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline produces a qualitatively distinctive psychedelic experience &mdash; often described as warmer, more body-engaged, and more visually elaborate than LSD, and longer than psilocybin. Experiences of colour intensification, geometric and figurative visual richness, and emotional depth are characteristic. Many practitioners describe mescaline as uniquely &ldquo;grounded&rdquo; &mdash; less likely to produce the ego-destabilising quality of high-dose psilocybin, more likely to produce a sense of earthy, embodied presence and connection.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Onset</h3>
          <p class="content-p">45&ndash;90 minutes with peyote (the alkaloid mixture slows absorption); somewhat faster with purified mescaline or San Pedro preparations. Nausea is common during onset, particularly with peyote.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Peak</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Reaches full intensity at 3&ndash;5 hours. At moderate doses: vivid visual enhancement, emotional warmth, sense of connection to the natural world and other beings. At higher doses: more pronounced perceptual alterations and potentially ego-dissolving experiences.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Duration</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Total duration 10&ndash;12 hours &mdash; longer than psilocybin, comparable to LSD. This demands careful scheduling and adequate energy reserves.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Dosage Reference -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-dosage">Dosage Reference</h2>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="dosage-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Pure Mescaline</th><th>Dried Peyote Buttons</th><th>Character</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Low / Threshold</td><td>100&ndash;150 mg</td><td>3&ndash;6 buttons</td><td>Mild perceptual shifts, mood elevation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Moderate</td><td>200&ndash;300 mg</td><td>6&ndash;10 buttons</td><td>Full mescaline experience</td></tr>
              <tr><td>High</td><td>300&ndash;500 mg</td><td>10&ndash;15 buttons</td><td>Intense; likely strong visuals and ego-dissolution</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Very high</td><td>500+ mg</td><td>15+ buttons</td><td>Profound; not recommended without extensive experience</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="content-p"><em>Peyote button potency varies considerably by source, age, and growing conditions. Dosing by button count is imprecise. San Pedro mescaline content also varies widely by plant and preparation method.</em></p>
        </div>

        <!-- Research -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-research">Research on Mescaline</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline has been less studied in contemporary clinical trials than psilocybin or MDMA, partly due to its longer duration making it more resource-intensive to study, and partly due to its complex cultural associations. However, survey and observational research is growing.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Peyote ceremony has been the subject of several longitudinal studies in Native American Church members. Research by Halpern and colleagues (2005) found no evidence of psychological or cognitive impairment in long-term peyote users, and some evidence of lower rates of alcohol and substance use disorders &mdash; findings consistent with the established therapeutic role of the ceremony in these communities.</p>
          <p class="content-p">More recent survey research by Uthaug and colleagues and others has found that mescaline use is associated with high rates of reported personal and spiritual significance, increased nature-connectedness, and positive psychological outcomes &mdash; though this literature shares the methodological limitations (self-selection, self-report, lack of controls) common to all survey-based psychedelic research.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Risks and Contraindications -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-risks">Risks and Contraindications</h2>
          <div class="content-callout">
            <div class="callout-label">&#9888;&#65039; Check your medications</div>
            <p>Mescaline shares the interaction profile of classical psychedelics &mdash; SSRIs, MAOIs, and lithium all carry meaningful risks that require review before use. <a href="/assessment/#ass-combochart">View full drug interaction chart &rarr;</a></p>
          </div>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline shares the standard psychological contraindications of classical psychedelics: personal or family history of schizophrenia spectrum disorders or bipolar I. The long duration (10&ndash;12 hours) means difficult experiences last significantly longer and demand greater preparation and support. Cardiovascular load is moderate &mdash; cardiac conditions warrant consultation.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Nausea is more common with mescaline (especially peyote) than with psilocybin or LSD, and is understood in traditional contexts as part of the purgative and healing process. Ginger, preparation through fasting, and accepting rather than resisting the nausea reduce its intensity.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Verifying What You Have -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-testing">Verifying What You Have</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline is unusual on this site in that most people take it as plant material rather than as a measured compound, which changes what verification means. Reagents have a role, but the harder questions are which cactus you have and how much mescaline is in the part of it you are about to use.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Powder sold as mescaline is usually not mescaline</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Start with the arithmetic, because it settles most cases without a test kit. As the <a href="#mes-dosage" class="inline-link">dosage table</a> shows, mescaline is active from around 100 mg and a full experience runs to 300&ndash;500 mg. That is a large amount of powder, several times what a typical capsule of anything else on this site contains. A small baggie or a single ordinary capsule cannot hold a mescaline dose. If someone has sold you a modest quantity of powder as mescaline, it is something else, and given the dose range the most likely candidates are far more potent compounds. Synthetic mescaline exists but is genuinely rare, because making it is more trouble than growing a cactus.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Where you do have powder, Marquis is the usual first reagent, producing an orange to brown reaction with mescaline. As always this identifies a family rather than a specific compound, and it will not distinguish mescaline from related phenethylamines. Given the dose arithmetic above, a reagent result should never override the quantity question.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Identifying the cactus</h3>
          <p class="content-p">The <em>Trichocereus</em> and <em>Echinopsis</em> group is taxonomically messy and nursery labelling is frequently wrong. San Pedro (<em>Echinopsis pachanoi</em>), Peruvian torch (<em>E. peruviana</em>), and Bolivian torch (<em>E. lageniformis</em>) all contain mescaline in differing amounts, and a number of visually similar ornamental columnar cacti contain little or none. Rib count, spine pattern, and areole spacing are the usual identifying features, and none of them is reliable enough from a photograph. Buying from a specialist grower who names the species and can say where the cutting came from removes most of this problem.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Concentration is not uniform, in any direction</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline content varies enormously between species, between individual plants of the same species, with age, and with growing conditions. It also varies within a single cactus: the mescaline is concentrated in the green photosynthetic tissue just beneath the skin, while the pale inner flesh and the woody core hold very little. This means the weight of a piece of cactus tells you almost nothing about its dose, because two pieces of identical weight can differ several-fold depending on how much green layer each contains.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The practical consequences are covered in the <a href="#mes-dosage" class="inline-link">dosage section</a>, and the important one is simple: with an unfamiliar plant, a conservative first amount is the only defensible approach, and a second session tells you far more than a scale does. Dried and powdered cactus bought online compounds every one of these uncertainties at once, since you can no longer see what species it was or which part of it you have.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Legal Status -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-legal">Legal Status</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Mescaline is Schedule I under the 1971 UN Convention, but the plants that produce it are not, and that gap does more work here than for almost any other substance. Across much of the world a mescaline-containing cactus is a legal ornamental plant while the compound inside it is a controlled substance, so growing one is lawful and preparing it for consumption is not.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>United States:</strong> mescaline is Schedule I, and peyote is separately listed by name. The exemption for Native American Church ceremonial use is written into federal regulation and reinforced by the 1994 amendment to the American Indian Religious Freedom Act, which protects use, possession, and transport by Native American practitioners for bona fide ceremonial purposes. San Pedro and related cacti are legal to buy and grow as ornamentals, and preparing them for consumption is not covered by that.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Canada:</strong> notably different. Mescaline is Schedule III under the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act, but the schedule explicitly excludes peyote, so the plant itself is not a controlled substance in Canada. Extracting or concentrating mescaline from it would be, since the isolated compound remains scheduled.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Mexico:</strong> Wix&aacute;rika ceremonial use is recognised, <em>Lophophora williamsii</em> is a legally protected species, and harvesting it outside that context is an offence against both drug law and conservation law.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> mescaline is Class A. Live cacti are legal, but drying or preparing them produces a Class A preparation.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The pattern to take away is that the plant and the compound are treated as separate things almost everywhere, and the act of preparing one into the other is where the legal line sits. See the <a href="/law/#law-table" class="inline-link">legal quick reference</a> for comparison.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- If You're Considering This -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="mes-considering">If You Are Considering This</h2>
          <p class="content-p">General preparation is on <a href="/assessment/" class="inline-link">assessment</a>, <a href="/preparation/" class="inline-link">preparation</a>, and <a href="/integration/" class="inline-link">integration</a>. Four things belong to mescaline specifically.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Choose San Pedro</h3>
          <p class="content-p">For a non-Indigenous reader this is the one decision that matters most, and it is not a close call. Peyote takes ten to thirty years to reach maturity, its wild populations are in genuine decline, and Indigenous practitioners have asked outsiders directly to leave it alone. San Pedro grows quickly, is cultivated everywhere as an ornamental, and carries the same compound. The <a href="#mes-ethics" class="inline-link">ethics section</a> sets out the full reasoning. The short version is that choosing San Pedro costs you nothing and choosing peyote costs someone else something real.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">This is a whole day and then some</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ten to fourteen hours of effects puts mescaline alongside LSD rather than psilocybin, and many people describe the descent as gradual rather than clean. Start in the morning. Clear the following day. A long duration also means a difficult passage lasts a long time, so the preparation that feels excessive for a four-hour experience is proportionate here.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Expect nausea and plan around it rather than through it</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Nausea is more common and more pronounced with mescaline than with psilocybin or LSD, particularly with whole plant material, and it usually arrives in the first two hours. Eating nothing substantial beforehand helps. Ginger helps. Reducing brewed cactus down to a smaller volume helps considerably, because a large quantity of bitter liquid is itself part of the problem. Traditional contexts treat the purge as part of the process rather than a malfunction, and resisting it tends to prolong it.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Treat every new plant as an unknown dose</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Everything in <a href="#mes-testing" class="inline-link">verifying what you have</a> means the amount of cactus you prepared last time is not a reliable guide to a different plant, or even to a different section of the same one. Begin low with any new material and accept that the first session with it is partly a calibration. That is a slower way to work and it is the only honest one available.</p>

          <p class="content-p">Mescaline rewards patience at every stage, from growing the plant to waiting out the nausea to sitting through a long afternoon. It is the oldest of these relationships and the least amenable to being hurried.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div class="content-section" id="mes-faq">
          <h2 class="content-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-list">
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q"><span>Can I attend a peyote ceremony as a non-Indigenous person?</span><span class="faq-icon">+</span></div>
              <div class="faq-a"><p>Native American Church ceremonies are generally not open to non-Indigenous participants &mdash; the church is a legally protected Indigenous religious institution, not a public service. Some Wix&aacute;ritari and other Mexican Indigenous guides do work with non-Indigenous participants, but genuine engagement with these traditions requires real relationship, preparation, and reciprocity &mdash; not a transaction. If you are called to mescaline, San Pedro and the huachuma tradition offer a more appropriate entry point for most non-Indigenous people.</p></div>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-q"><span>Is San Pedro legal?</span><span class="faq-icon">+</span></div>
              <div class="faq-a"><p>San Pedro cacti are legal to cultivate and sell as ornamental plants in most jurisdictions, including Canada and the United States &mdash; their legal status is similar to poppies, which are legal to grow but illegal to process into opiates. Extracting or consuming mescaline from San Pedro is illegal in most jurisdictions where mescaline is a controlled substance. The legal line is typically drawn at the point of extraction or preparation for consumption.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

